<Layout title="Financial Transitions | RIPL Wealth" description="A practical approach to financial decisions after a business sale, retirement, inheritance, divorce, loss of a spouse or sudden liquidity.">
<section class="hero"><div class="wrap"><div class="eyebrow">FINANCIAL TRANSITIONS</div><h1>Something changed. Your financial plan probably needs to change with it.</h1><p class="small">By RIPL Wealth · Updated September 30, 2026</p><p class="lead">Selling a business. Retiring. Receiving an inheritance. Divorce. Losing a spouse. A sudden increase in wealth. Big financial changes can create a lot of decisions at once.</p><p class="answer"><strong>You probably don't need to make all of them today.</strong></p><a class="btn" href="#what-next">Figure Out What Comes Next </a></div></section>
<section class="section" id="what-next"><div class="wrap article"><h2>First, let's figure out what actually needs your attention.</h2><p>When something significant changes financially, there's a natural temptation to start making decisions. Move the money. Invest it. Pay something off. Change accounts. Buy something. Sell something.</p><p>Sometimes those decisions are necessary. <strong>Sometimes the smartest decision is giving yourself enough time to understand what just changed.</strong></p><p>My first job is to help you separate the decisions that need to happen now from the ones that can wait. Then we can understand what you have, what you need and what you want this next chapter of your financial life to look like.</p></div></section>
<section class="section questions"><div class="wrap"><div class="grid transition-grid">{cards.map(([h,p,u],i)=><a class="card" href={u}><span class="num">0{i+1}</span><h3>{h}</h3><p>{p}</p></a>)}</div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap"><h2>Not every decision has the same deadline.</h2><div class="decision-grid"><div><span>NOW</span><h3>What genuinely needs attention?</h3><p>Taxes, deadlines, cash needs, required distributions, legal or administrative matters, insurance and immediate obligations.</p></div><div><span>NEXT</span><h3>What needs a plan, but not an answer today?</h3><p>Cash allocation, debt, investment strategy, retirement income, account consolidation and tax planning.</p></div><div><span>LATER</span><h3>What deserves thought once the dust settles?</h3><p>Long-term investing, gifting, helping family, charitable giving, major purchases, legacy and what you want your wealth to make possible.</p></div></div><div class="callout"><h3>Good financial planning isn't always about making decisions faster.</h3><p>Sometimes it's knowing which decisions don't need to be made yet.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section dark"><div class="wrap split"><div><div class="eyebrow">LIQUIDITY BUYS TIME</div><h2>Sometimes cash has a very important job: buying you time.</h2></div><div><p>After a major financial transition, keeping some money liquid isn't necessarily lazy money. It may be doing exactly what you need it to do.</p><p>Cash can give you room to understand taxes, establish spending needs, evaluate opportunities, make thoughtful investment decisions and adjust to a financial life that may look very different than it did six months ago.</p><p><strong>Money isn't lazy simply because it's sitting in cash. It's lazy when we can't explain why it's there.</strong></p></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="wrap article"><h2>Eventually, every dollar needs its next job.</h2><p>Some money may stay liquid. Some may provide income. Some may continue growing. Some may reduce debt, help your children, support charity or become part of your legacy.</p><p class="answer"><strong>The transition changed your financial life. Now we decide what the money needs to do next.</strong></p><a class="text-link dark-link" href="/capital-clarity/">See How We Think About Capital Clarity </a></div></section>
<section class="final-cta"><div class="wrap"><h2>You don't need to have everything figured out before we talk.</h2><p>In fact, that's probably the point. Tell us what changed, what you're thinking about and what you're worried you might be missing. We'll figure out what actually needs to happen next.</p><a class="btn" href="/contact/">Start the Conversation </a> <a class="text-link dark-link cta-secondary" href="/money-questions/">Explore Financial Transition Questions </a></div></section>
</Layout>
